---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'FAQ | lemonlawcalc.com';
const description = 'Frequently asked questions about lemon law claims: what qualifies, repair attempts, mileage offsets, and attorney fees.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
  <h2>What counts as a &ldquo;substantial defect&rdquo;?</h2>
  <p>A defect that significantly impairs the vehicle&rsquo;s use, value, or safety — not a minor cosmetic issue. Examples: engine problems, transmission failures, braking issues, electrical system failures, and air conditioning failures in hot climates. Cosmetic defects and defects caused by owner misuse are generally excluded.</p>
  <h2>How many repair attempts are required?</h2>
  <p>Varies by state. Common thresholds: 3–4 attempts for the same non-safety defect; 1–2 attempts for safety-related defects; or 30 cumulative days out of service (regardless of the number of separate issues). Some states use a combination of these tests.</p>
  <h2>What is the mileage offset?</h2>
  <p>The refund for a lemon law repurchase is not always 100% of the purchase price. Most states deduct a mileage offset for miles driven before the defect first arose. California uses a formula: (miles before defect / 120,000) × purchase price. Some states do not allow mileage offsets.</p>
  <h2>Are used vehicles covered?</h2>
  <p>State lemon laws typically cover new vehicles. Used vehicle coverage varies: some states cover certified pre-owned vehicles still under the original manufacturer&rsquo;s warranty; others do not. The federal Magnuson-Moss Warranty Act may provide additional rights for used vehicles sold with written warranties.</p>
  <h2>Do I need to hire an attorney?</h2>
  <p>Most state lemon laws require manufacturers to pay the consumer&rsquo;s attorney fees if the consumer wins. This means you can hire a lemon law attorney at no out-of-pocket cost in most successful cases. Attorneys who specialize in lemon law typically take cases on a fee-shifting basis, not contingency.</p>
</BaseLayout>
